# Length as a Primary Confound in AI-vs-Human Stylistic Comparisons: A Conservative Preliminary Report

## Abstract

**Keywords:** stylometry, AI-generated text, computational stylistics, lexical diversity, conjunction frequency, Random Forest classifier, creative writing, multi-model comparison

Do AI-generated text and human-written text differ systematically in structural style, and if so, which differences hold across diverse language models? This preliminary report quantifies stylistic differences using eight locked structural metrics (word length, sentence length, sentence length variance, type-token ratio, MTLD, conjunction frequency, paragraph length, and paragraph length variance), plus five supplementary indicators (lexical density, word-length standard deviation, long-word ratio, sentence-length coefficient of variation, and sentences per paragraph; Section 3.5), applied to 30 creative writing prompts, each paired with one human-authored story and AI-generated outputs from 9 contemporary models, namely GPT-3.5, MiMo-V2.5-Pro, DeepSeek-V4-Flash, MiniMax-M3, Muse-Spark-1.2-Contributor, Kimi-K3, Gemini-3.6-Flash, GPT-5.6-Luna and Claude-Sonnet-5-High, yielding 299 observations. We compare each model's 30 texts against the 30 human texts with independent-samples Welch t-tests (two-tailed; no multiplicity correction; Cohen's d reported for every comparison), quantify within-model homogeneity across the nine core indicators (the eight locked metrics plus MATTR; variance ratios and Levene tests), and train a Random Forest classifier evaluated with StratifiedGroupKFold cross-validation grouped by prompt. AI-generated texts are systematically **1.6× longer** than human-written texts in our sample (mean 719 vs 439 words). AI texts also show lower within-model variance than the human group in most metric comparisons — most consistently in the lexical and paragraph layers, such as MATTR, TTR, conjunction frequency and paragraph-length variance (smaller variance in 9/9 models; median variance ratios 0.08–0.56). Length residualization shows that the apparent vocabulary poverty of AI texts is length-attributable: the raw AI-lower TTR pattern (5/9 conditions) does not survive as such — the 4/9 conditions that remain significant after length control reverse to AI > human, aligning TTR with MATTR and MTLD. Conjunction-frequency differences partially persist (5/9 survive pooled residualization; 2/9 under an ANCOVA robustness check). The eight-metric classifier achieves 91.6% raw accuracy but only 68.7% balanced accuracy (MCC = 0.449, AUC = 0.867, human recall 12/30 = 40%), **essentially tied with a length-only baseline** at 69.4% balanced accuracy. **Findings are preliminary and underpowered for medium effects** (~30% power at r=0.3); acquisition-channel heterogeneity across four sources, namely direct API, third-party dataset, Manual Arena paste and Faro API, precludes model-level interpretation. Specific items this paper cannot confirm are listed in the Limitations section.

## 1. Introduction

Large language models have transformed how text is produced across education, journalism, creative writing, and professional communication. As AI-generated text becomes ubiquitous, a natural question arises: does AI writing exhibit systematic structural patterns that distinguish it from human writing? This question matters not only for detection but also for understanding what AI writing "sounds like" at a structural level, independent of content or coherence.

A common observation is that AI-generated text tends to be "patterned" or "formulaic" in its structure, with similar sentence lengths, uniform paragraph structures, and consistent use of transition words. However, these observations have rarely been subjected to rigorous empirical testing across multiple AI systems using controlled, prompt-matched data.

Most existing work on AI text characterization has focused on semantic coherence, factual accuracy, or surface-level detection. Fewer studies have employed computational stylometry, quantitative analysis of writing style features, to systematically compare AI and human text. Stylometric methods have a long history in authorship attribution and forensic linguistics (Juola, 2008), and they offer a principled framework for identifying structural patterns that persist across topics and genres.

This study sits at the intersection of two strands of prior work. First, recent applications of stylometry reach high accuracy in distinguishing human from AI creative writing (O'Sullivan, 2025). Corpus work on prompt-matched stories, in particular the GPT-WritingPrompts collection, documents systematic human-versus-GPT-3.5 differences in character portrayal (Huang et al., 2024); our human baseline and prompt set come from the parent WritingPrompts corpus (Fan et al., 2018), and the shorter stories in the GPT-3.5 condition (531 ± 40 words) reflect that collection's 500-word instruction rather than a model property. Second, studies of LLM creativity find that AI prose is fluent but comparatively lacking in idiosyncratic style (Chakrabarty et al., 2024a) and that writers edit model output to inject personal voice (Chakrabarty et al., 2024b), while genre conventions further shape model output (Lucy & Bamman, 2021); together these findings suggest that AI text may be more uniform than human text, the prediction that our variance-based tests target directly.

A second strand concerns measurement. Vocabulary-richness indices differ in how they treat text length: raw type-token ratio is length-sensitive, which motivated length-controlled alternatives such as MTLD (McCarthy & Jarvis, 2010) and MATTR with a moving window (Covington & McFall, 2010; the window is fixed at 100 tokens in this study). Because length covaries with stylistic variables more broadly, comparisons that ignore it risk reporting the statistics of length rather than of style. Within our hypothesis set, conjunction frequency and vocabulary diversity have the strongest prior support, whereas the evidence on sentence- and paragraph-length variance is more mixed; the report therefore tests four directional hypotheses plus one classification benchmark (H5) across nine contemporary models.

The present study addresses this gap by comparing eight locked structural metrics across 9 contemporary AI models and human-authored stories, all written in response to the same 30 creative writing prompts. This design controls for topic and genre effects through prompt matching, compares each model's outputs against the human baseline as independent groups, and combines hypothesis-driven testing with machine learning classification.

Our study makes three contributions. First, it examines multiple AI systems rather than treating "AI text" as monolithic. Second, it uses a prompt-matched design that controls for prompt-level confounds. Third, it combines independent-samples group tests for specific directional hypotheses with Random Forest classification for overall discriminability.

The analysis focuses on creative short fiction from Reddit's r/WritingPrompts, a genre where structural variation is naturally high and where AI writing assistance is increasingly common. By quantifying which structural stereotypes hold empirically and which do not, this study provides a more nuanced, evidence-based understanding of how AI writing differs from human writing.

## 2. Method

### 2.1 Data

The study uses 30 writing prompts from the Reddit WritingPrompts corpus (Fan et al., 2018). For each prompt, we collected one human-authored story and one AI-generated story from each of 9 AI models, yielding up to 30 prompt-matched observations per condition.

Human baseline. We use 30 human-written stories from the WritingPrompts dataset, as processed by Huang et al. (2024), licensed under MIT.

AI conditions. The 9 AI models represent a range of architectures, training approaches, and generation dates:

| Model | Provider | Source | N |
|-------|----------|--------|---|
| GPT-3.5 | OpenAI | Huang et al. (2024) | 30 |
| MiMo-V2.5-Pro | Xiaomi | Command Code Goat | 30 |
| DeepSeek-V4-Flash | DeepSeek | Command Code Goat | 30 |
| MiniMax-M3 | MiniMax | MiniMax CN (Anthropic) | 30 |
| Muse-Spark-1.2 | Muse | Command Code Goat | 30 |
| Kimi-K3 | Moonshot | Command Code Goat | 29 |
| Gemini-3.6-Flash | Google | Arena + Faro API | 30 |
| GPT-5.6-Luna | OpenAI | Manual Arena | 30 |
| Claude-Sonnet-5-High | Anthropic | Faro API | 30 |

Kimi-K3 is missing 1 observation (prompt 26, "best friend speaks in rhyme", repeatedly timed out across multiple API providers). All other AI conditions have complete N=30 coverage.

### 2.2 Metrics

We extract eight locked structural metrics from each text using spaCy (en_core_web_sm) for tokenization, sentence splitting, and POS tagging, plus custom Python functions for paragraph analysis. Five supplementary indicators at the word, sentence and paragraph levels (Section 3.5) are analysed separately and are not part of the locked set.

| # | Metric | Definition | Implementation |
|---|--------|------------|----------------|
| 1 | Word length | Mean characters per word | Average of `len(token.text)` over spaCy tokens |
| 2 | Sentence length | Mean words per sentence | Average token count per spaCy sentence |
| 3 | Sentence length variance | Population variance of sentence lengths | `numpy.var` over sentence word counts |
| 4 | TTR | Type-token ratio | Unique tokens / total tokens |
| 5 | MTLD | Measure of Textual Lexical Diversity | Implementation following McCarthy and Jarvis (2010) |
| 6 | Conjunction frequency | Conjunctions per word | Count of POS tags CCONJ and SCONJ / total words |
| 7 | Paragraph length | Mean words per paragraph | Average word count per paragraph (split on double newline) |
| 8 | Paragraph length variance | Population variance of paragraph lengths | `numpy.var` over paragraph word counts |

### 2.3 Hypotheses

We test 4 directional hypotheses using condition-level group comparisons, in which each AI condition's texts are compared as one group against the 30 human texts:

| Hypothesis | Metric | Direction | Rationale |
|------------|--------|-----------|-----------|
| H1 | Sentence length variance | AI < Human | AI text uses more uniform sentence lengths |
| H2 | Paragraph length variance | AI < Human | AI text uses more uniform paragraph lengths |
| H3 | TTR | AI < Human | AI text is more repetitive in vocabulary |
| H4 | Conjunction frequency | AI > Human | AI text uses more explicit connectives |

H5 tests whether a Random Forest classifier using all eight locked metrics achieves balanced accuracy significantly above the 50% chance baseline (permutation test, p<0.05, n_perm=1000), evaluated with 5-fold StratifiedGroupKFold cross-validation (group=prompt_id).

### 2.4 Statistical Analysis

Hypothesis testing. Condition-level comparisons are performed as independent-samples group comparisons: for each AI condition, the model's 30 texts form one group and the 30 human texts form the other, and we apply Welch's independent-samples t-test (two-tailed). No multiple-comparison correction is applied; exact p-values are reported together with Cohen's d for every comparison, and the two should be read jointly — with larger samples, small fluctuations can push p below .05, so the effect size carries the interpretive weight. As a robustness check, every comparison is also run as a Mann-Whitney U (rank-sum) test; across the 81 metric × condition comparisons the two approaches agree at α = 0.05 in 72 cases (89%). MATTR is computed with a fixed window of 100; no cross-window comparison is performed, and window-sensitivity checks are reported as a robustness note only.

Classification (H5). We train a Random Forest classifier (scikit-learn, 100 trees, random_state=42, class_weight='balanced') using all eight locked metrics as features. To prevent data leakage from prompt-level grouping, since the same prompt generates both human and AI observations, we evaluate using 5-fold StratifiedGroupKFold cross-validation (group=prompt_id, shuffle=True, random_state=42). We report mean accuracy plus or minus standard deviation across folds, per-condition accuracy, and feature importance rankings. We also report the majority-class baseline, which predicts the majority class for all observations, as a reference point for interpreting classifier accuracy. Statistical significance of the classifier's balanced accuracy is assessed via a group-aware permutation test (n_perm=1000): we shuffle labels within each prompt_id group, retrain the classifier, and compute the proportion of permuted balanced accuracies that meet or exceed the observed value (one-tailed p-value).

Tools. All analyses use Python 3 with spaCy (Honnibal & Montani, 2017) for tokenization and part-of-speech tagging, pandas, numpy, scipy, and scikit-learn (Pedregosa et al., 2011) for the Random Forest classifier. All analysis code is listed in the Data and Code Availability section.

Software Environment. All experiments were conducted using Python 3.11 with the following package versions: spaCy 3.8.0 (with `en_core_web_sm` 3.8.0), scikit-learn 1.6.0, pandas 2.3.3, numpy 1.26.4, and scipy 1.17.1. Random seeds are set throughout the pipeline: `random.seed(42)` for prompt sampling, and `random_state=42` for the Random Forest classifier and cross-validation splits. This enables full reproducibility of all reported statistics on the same hardware and software environment for the six controlled API conditions; the GPT-3.5, Gemini-3.6-Flash, and GPT-5.6-Luna conditions are snapshot-based and not regenerable from the documentation alone.

Tokenization operationalization. Word length, TTR, and conjunction frequency use only alphabetic tokens (token.is_alpha=True), excluding punctuation, numbers, and whitespace. Word length is the mean character count of alphabetic tokens. TTR is the ratio of unique lowercased alphabetic tokens to total alphabetic tokens, without lemmatization. Conjunction frequency is the count of CCONJ and SCONJ tags among alphabetic tokens divided by total alphabetic tokens. Re-running with this filter yields word length means within ±0.02 characters of the original, for example Human 4.27 versus filtered 4.27, confirming the original analysis is not polluted by punctuation.

MTLD Implementation Note. The MTLD metric was implemented following the algorithm described in McCarthy and Jarvis (2010), using a TTR threshold of 0.72 and segment size of 10. The implementation computes forward and backward pass MTLD and reports their mean. Independent replication using the `lexical_diversity` Python package is recommended for confirmation studies; that package provides the original authors' reference implementation. MATTR Implementation Note. MATTR, the moving-average type–token ratio, follows Covington and McFall (2010), who introduce the moving-window algorithm and show that the resulting measure is independent of text length. The window size is fixed at 100 tokens for every text and every condition; because MATTR values depend on the window size, the window is held constant rather than tuned.

