### 3.4 Word Length: A Difference Not Explained by Length

Word length (characters per word) is not among the pre-specified hypotheses; it is reported here as an exploratory finding because it is the one dimension on which group differences are not eliminated by length control. Group comparisons are significant in 3 of 9 conditions, namely GPT-3.5, GPT-5.6-Luna and Gemini-3.6-Flash, all with AI > human (Table 2a), and the AI groups' word-length variance is smaller than the human group's in 8/9 conditions. Notably, word length is the metric whose differences are *not* eliminated by length control: after residualizing on word count, significant differences increase to 5 of 9 conditions, namely GPT-3.5, MiMo-V2.5-Pro, GPT-5.6-Luna, Claude-Sonnet-5-High and Gemini-3.6-Flash, all with AI > human, and an ANCOVA robustness check retains 3 of 9, namely GPT-3.5, MiniMax-M3 and Gemini-3.6-Flash. If anything, text length masks the word-length difference: AI texts are longer, and longer texts use shorter words (r = −0.17 between word length and word count), which dilutes the raw contrast.

