## Limitations

Several limitations constrain the generalizability of these findings. First, the sample size is moderate (N=30 prompts per condition), which limits statistical power for detecting small effects. Given n=30 prompts and observed effect sizes, we have approximately 80% power to detect r > 0.5 (large effects by Cohen 1988) at α=0.05; power for r=0.3 is approximately 30%, supporting the planned N=90 expansion for medium-effect inference. Second, the study analyzes only English creative fiction from a single corpus (WritingPrompts). Results may differ for other genres or languages. Third, AI models evolve rapidly; the structural signatures identified here reflect specific model versions and may not persist in future releases. Fourth, the classifier uses StratifiedGroupKFold cross-validation (grouped by prompt) to avoid data leakage from prompt-level grouping, but the class imbalance (269 AI observations vs. 30 human) means that accuracy must be interpreted against the majority-class baseline. Fifth, statistical inference follows an independent-samples group design (no multiplicity correction; §2.4); exact p-values are reported together with Cohen's d throughout, and readers should weigh effect sizes alongside significance. The length-control analyses are method-sensitive in places (pooled residualization vs. ANCOVA; §3.2) and both are reported. Sixth, the word length finding (3/9 raw significant; 5/9 after length control) was not part of the pre-specified hypotheses and requires independent replication. Seventh, Kimi-K3 is missing 1 observation (prompt 26, "best friend speaks in rhyme", which repeatedly timed out across multiple API providers due to the difficulty of generating sustained rhyming text). Eighth, AI texts are systematically longer than human texts in this dataset (mean 531-878 words vs 439 words), and MTLD has a moderate within-condition correlation with word count (mean r=0.31). The length-matched subsample analysis (400-800 words) was inconclusive on whether MTLD's discriminating power is independent of text length, because the 400-800 word subsample retained only 12/30 human texts, limiting the power of this robustness check. Future work should use regression-based length control or exact length-matching designs. Ninth, model identity on third-party aggregation endpoints (Command Code Goat, Faro API) is not independently verified; our results assume the endpoint labels match the underlying model versions. This limitation does not affect the 6 conditions we control fully, namely MiMo, DeepSeek, MiniMax, Muse, Kimi and GPT-3.5 via a documented dataset, but applies to GPT-5.6, Claude-Sonnet-5-High, and partially Gemini-3.6-Flash.

Specific items that remain unconfirmed:

1. Statistical power: For medium effects (r=0.3), statistical power is approximately 30%; a non-significant result after length control does not formally establish absence of effect; TOST equivalence tests are recommended but not conducted here.
2. MTLD importance ranking: the feature-importance value for MTLD (0.340) has not been verified under the lexical_diversity reference implementation of the metric; re-running the classifier with that implementation remains to be done.
3. Acquisition-channel confound: 4 distinct acquisition sources, namely direct API, third-party dataset, Manual Arena paste and Faro API, preclude model-level claims; the paper's findings apply to the 9 pipelines as a whole, not to models as such.
4. Strength of claims: we avoid "clean refutation" language; conclusions state either "no detectable effect" or "partial survival under length control", as appropriate.
5. Human baseline variance: 30 human stories (1 per prompt) provides no within-prompt variance estimate; the human baseline's length distribution (439 ± 245 words) suggests substantial heterogeneity that may affect the AI-vs-human group comparisons.
6. Per-condition length-only baselines: Table 3 reports per-condition balanced accuracy for the eight-metric classifier but not for a length-only baseline at the same per-condition level; the +6.6pp raw accuracy advantage and the +0.114 MCC advantage of the 8-metric model over a global length-only baseline do not necessarily hold at the per-condition level.
7. Kimi-K3 missing observation: 1 observation missing (prompt 26, a rhyme task); the missingness mechanism may be MNAR (missing-not-at-random) and no sensitivity analysis was conducted.

Headline takeaway: AI-generated creative text is 1.6× longer than human-written text in our sample; under length control, vocabulary-diversity differences are length-attributable (surviving differences reverse to AI-higher), while conjunction-frequency differences partially persist. The 8-metric classifier does not exceed a length-only baseline on balanced accuracy. These findings are preliminary, underpowered for medium effects, and confounded by acquisition-channel heterogeneity; they should not be interpreted as a definitive refutation of any pre-existing hypothesis without TOST equivalence testing and replication at N=90.

## Future Work

Future iterations should expand to N=90 (3 batches of 30 prompts), providing adequate power for detecting medium effects and enabling formal Bonferroni correction. The N=90 design should include a regression-based length control: residualize MTLD and TTR after regressing on word count, then re-run the Random Forest classifier on length-residualized features to establish whether vocabulary diversity differences survive strict length adjustment. An exact length-matching design (restricting to a narrow word-count band where both human and AI texts have adequate representation) would provide complementary evidence. Cross-linguistic analysis would test whether the conjunction frequency pattern holds in languages with different connective systems. Extension to additional registers such as academic writing, news articles and conversational text would determine whether these structural differences are genre-specific or generalizable. Word length should be formally registered as a pre-registered hypothesis (H6) in future work, given its significant elevation in 3 of 9 models (raw) and 5 of 9 after length control; the post-hoc nature of this finding underscores the importance of pre-registration to distinguish confirmatory from exploratory analyses. The method sensitivity of the H4 length-control result (5/9 pooled residualization vs. 2/9 ANCOVA) illustrates the importance of pre-specified analysis choices. Investigating why Gemini-3.6-Flash uniquely avoids the conjunction overuse pattern could clarify what model-specific factors shape stylistic fingerprints.

